# The durable Danwa conversation workflow: one addressed message becomes one
# conversation turn, and one journal generation holds exactly one turn.
#
# The hand-owned runtime is Shikigami.Agent.Conversation in shikigami-core.
#
# This body is ordered for reading; replay matches journal records by label, so
# it is the label inventory of every path through the workflow, not a
# control-flow program.
#
# Why the labels are static now. A keiro journal matches records to steps by
# label, so two turns in one generation must not both write `record-user`. The
# old body ran every turn of a conversation in one immortal generation, which is
# why every per-message label carried a computed `:<messageId>` suffix — and a
# workflow label is a compile-time wire word ([A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*), so a
# colon kept the whole turn body out of this specification. Rotating onto a
# fresh generation after every turn removes the constraint at its root: a
# generation holds one turn, so each label is written at most once and needs no
# suffix. That is what makes the body below declarable and puts it under
# `just keiro-gate` for the first time.
#
# Not declared here, and why:
#
#   * The legacy loop's per-message labels — `record-user:<messageId>`,
#     `run-behavior:<messageId>`, `run-behavior-v2:<messageId>`,
#     `record-assistant-and-reply:<messageId>`, `turn-result:<messageId>` — and
#     its per-turn await `next-message:<ordinal>`. They still exist: they are
#     the False branch of `shikigami-conversation-rotation-v1`, which is what
#     keeps an instance parked under the old shape coherent. A label this
#     specification cannot hold is genuinely free, because there is no declared
#     body for the differ to compare; see
#     docs/adr/0012-a-declared-workflow-body-only-grows-behind-a-patch.md.
#   * `danwa-reply:<messageId>`, which is a sink-intent step name rather than a
#     journaled step — it names the outbox row, and nothing appends it to the
#     journal.
language keiro-dsl 5
context shikigami
layout collocated

workflow DanwaConversation
  name "shikigami.danwa-conversation"
  # Shikigami.Agent.Conversation.ConversationStart.
  in ConversationStart {
    agentName:Text
  }
  out ConversationTurnResult
  # WorkflowId is "danwa-conversation:" <> the initial message's conversationId
  # (Shikigami.Agent.Conversation.conversationWorkflowId), so it derives from
  # the whole input rather than from one declared field.
  id from input via conversationWorkflowId
  body
    step ingest-start -> ConversationStart
    step lookup-agent -> AgentDecl
    step resolve-session -> SessionId
    # Plan 17's guard. Its labels are the legacy loop's
    # `run-behavior-v2:<messageId>` — stable per message but undeclarable
    # because the suffix is computed and contains a colon — so this checked
    # block stays intentionally empty. The rotating branch below journals the
    # static `run-behavior-v2` instead and always uses attempt semantics, so it
    # does not read this guard.
    patch shikigami-behavior-retry-v1 {
    }
    # One turn per generation, and with it every static label.
    #
    # The guard is not decoration and the block's contiguity is not a style
    # choice. `keiro-dsl diff` classifies ANY change to a declared workflow body
    # as BREAKING unless the whole change is contained in a NEW patch guard
    # (appends included) or is a terminal `continueAsNew`; `stripNewPatches`
    # removes this block wholesale before comparing, so an edit outside it
    # re-breaks the comparison. Shikigami.Agent.Conversation reads the guard
    # once per interpreted run: True takes the rotating shape below, False keeps
    # the immortal loop and its suffixed labels, so an instance already in
    # flight replays the shape its journal was written under. Delete the guard,
    # the False branch, and this block together once no pre-change instance can
    # still resume; see docs/workflow-versioning.md.
    patch shikigami-conversation-rotation-v1 {
      step record-user -> RecordedTurn
      step run-behavior-v2 -> BehaviorOutput
      step record-assistant-and-reply -> AssistantTurnAndReply
      step turn-result -> ConversationTurnResult
      # One awakeable, two signalers. The ingress path signals it with the next
      # human message; the idle-timeout timer signals the same awakeable with a
      # timeout. Whichever arrives first wins and the loser is a no-op, because
      # signalAwakeable returns False on an already-completed awakeable.
      await next-message -> ConversationWake
      # The epoch's terminal step, taken only on the timeout arm: it completes
      # the conversation's kioku session and the workflow returns. Journaled
      # rather than done on the way out, because closing the session is an
      # external effect and a replay must return the recorded answer instead of
      # re-issuing the command.
      step idle-timeout -> SessionId
    }
    # Terminal, and therefore never inside a patch block: continueAsNew unwinds
    # the body, so it is the last thing the body can do.
    continueAsNew ConversationSeed

operation RunDanwaConversation
  run DanwaConversation
    input ConversationStart
    outcome -> ConversationTurnResult
